<<Problem 2: List Reversal 


<<blueprint
<<blueprint
    <<functional-correctness

        requires:
            (i) lst is a finite list of elements of type 'a
            (ii) no assumptions about ordering, distinctness, or equality of elements

        ensures:
            Let r be the returned list.
            (i) length r = length lst
            (ii) r contains exactly the same multiset of elements as lst
            (iii) ∀ i where 0 ≤ i < length lst, nth(r, i) = nth(lst, (length lst − 1 − i))
            (v) algorithm always terminates for all finite lists

    functional-correctness>>
blueprint>>

blueprint>>


<<operational steps
<<operational steps
    Two approaches are implemented as required: a naive version and an efficient version.

Naive: Recursively reverse the tail of the list and append the head to the end.
        1. If lst = [], return [].
        2. If lst = x :: xs,
            • recursively call reverse_naive on xs
            • append [x] to the end of the reversed tail result
        3. Return the final list.

    Complexity:
        The append (@) operation takes time proportional to the length of its left operand.
        For a list of length n, this leads to:
            n + (n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 1 = Θ(n²)

Efficient: Use an accumulator and prepend elements to collect the reversed list in linear time.
        1. Start with an empty accumulator acc = [].
        2. While lst = x :: xs,
            • move x from lst and prepend it to acc → acc becomes x :: acc
            • continue recursion on xs
        3. When lst becomes [], return acc.

    Complexity:
        Each recursive step consumes one element and performs O(1) work.
        Therefore total time = Θ(n) and space = Θ(1) extra.

Complexity Testing: When input size is doubled:
            • naive version runtime grows by ~4× → confirming Θ(n²)
            • efficient version runtime grows by ~2× → confirming Θ(n)

operational steps>>



<<ocaml code
(* Naive implementation: Θ(n²) *)
let rec reverse_naive (lst : 'a list) : 'a list =
  match lst with
  | [] -> []
  | x :: xs -> (reverse_naive xs) @ [x]

(* Efficient tail-recursive implementation: Θ(n) *)
let reverse (lst : 'a list) : 'a list =
  let rec reverse_helper (lst : 'a list) (acc : 'a list) : 'a list =
    match lst with
    | [] -> acc
    | x :: xs -> reverse_helper xs (x :: acc)
  in
  reverse_helper lst []
ocaml code>>

<<proof

    <<invariant
        Initialisation:
            reverse_helper [] acc = acc. Since an empty list reversed is still empty,
            and acc already contains the reversed portion accumulated so far,
            the invariant holds initially.

        Maintenance:
            Assume the invariant holds for reverse_helper tail acc, meaning:
                reverse_helper tail acc = (reverse of tail) @ acc.
            Now consider lst = head :: tail. The helper is called as:
                reverse_helper tail (head :: acc).
            Because head is added to the front of acc, head will appear at the end
            of the reversed tail when the full reversal is complete.
            Therefore the invariant remains valid after each recursive step.

        Termination:
            Each recursive call receives a strictly shorter list (the tail of lst).
            Since lst is finite, the sequence of recursive calls must eventually reach [].
            At that point the helper returns acc without further recursion.
            Hence the reverse function terminates for all finite lists.
    invariant>>



    <<induction

        base case:
            For lst = [], reverse_helper [] acc = acc.
            When reverse lst is called, the accumulator starts as [].
            Therefore the final result is [] — the correct reversal of the empty list.

        inductive hypothesis:
            Assume for any list of length k, the helper satisfies:
                reverse_helper tail acc = (reverse of tail) @ acc.

        inductive step:
            Consider a list of length k + 1: lst = head :: tail.
            The reverse function performs:
                reverse_helper tail (head :: acc).
            By the inductive hypothesis:
                reverse_helper tail (head :: acc)
                = (reverse of tail) @ (head :: acc).
            Appending head to the end of the reversed tail places head in the last position
            of the overall result, exactly as required when reversing lst.
            Therefore reverse (head :: tail) yields the completely reversed list.

    induction>>

proof>>

